{"id":"temporary-for-years","title":"Temporary for four years or more","tier":"REDFLAG","question":"How many people have been paid on a temporary exempt appointment, at half time or more, in the same job class and department for four fiscal years running?","finding":"816 people were paid as temporary exempt employees for at least 1,040 hours a year in the same class and department for four or more consecutive fiscal years since 2017; 258 of those runs were still going in fiscal 2026. The largest groups are Special Nurse (class P103) in Public Health (169), Public Svc Aide-Public Works (class 9916) in Public Works (57) and Custodian (class 2708) in City Administrator (25).","how":"City payroll file, fiscal 2017 to 2026. A run is a set of consecutive fiscal years in which the same name is paid 1,040 hours or more (half time) in the same class and department with the employment type Temporary Exempt. Listed by class and department: people with a run of four years or more, the longest run, and those whose run reaches fiscal 2026. Individuals are not listed.","limits":"The civil grand jury wrote in 2023 that some temporary exempt workers may serve up to three years under what it called Category 18 of the Charter’s exemptions, and that the City gave pandemic hires a further year; the payroll does not say which category anyone is in, and other categories may carry other terms. A long run is therefore a question for the exempt-appointment reports, not a breach shown by the payroll. Some classes are set up as temporary: in fiscal 2026 the payroll shows 100.0% of the hours in the largest group here, Special Nurse (class P103), as temporary exempt, which points to a standing as-needed class rather than a time-limited post. Two people with one name in the same class and department would be counted as one. What Human Resources itself reports as past the limit is in the screen “Exempt appointments as Human Resources reports them”.","columns":[{"k":"department","t":"Department","t